{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/item_completed","completed_at_ms":1790301051244,"item":{"content":[{"text":"Item 5 is complete and merged:\n\n- Annals [PR #2](https://github.com/evintunador/annals/pull/2): concise reports by default.\n- Annals [PR #4](https://github.com/evintunador/annals/pull/4): correct sync/pre-push guidance.\n- cledger [PR #21](https://github.com/evintunador/conversation-ledger/pull/21): explicit `scan --report` and `sync --report`.\n- Clean-clone verification: cledger 101/101; Annals 161 passed, one optional gitleaks check skipped.\n- Publication remains deferred.\n\nThe first remaining item is final-session tail capture.\n\nToday, `Stop`/`SessionEnd` capture to the current EOF, but Claude or Qwen can append closing records afterward. A later session sweeps that tail, but the final session in a project can remain incomplete indefinitely. A blocking delay cannot solve this—the host writes those records only after the hook returns.\n\nThe recommended design is:\n\n- Keep immediate synchronous capture.\n- On `SessionEnd`, launch a cledger-owned detached finalizer.\n- Wait 500 ms, then recapture after changes until the transcript is quiet for one second, capped at ten seconds.\n- Preserve the original Git anchor so a quick post-exit commit cannot mis-anchor the tail.\n- Keep the existing sweep as crash recovery.\n\nNative `\"async\": true` is insufficient: Claude documents that unfinished async hooks can be killed during headless teardown and recommends full detachment for work that must outlive the session; Qwen scopes async children to its own process. [Claude hooks reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks#run-hooks-in-the-background), [Qwen hooks documentation](https://qwenlm.github.io/qwen-code-docs/en/users/features/hooks/).\n\nI need two design decisions:\n\n1. Ship Claude first, validate it live, then implement Qwen separately? This is my recommendation because Claude has directly observed missing-tail evidence; Qwen has the same risk but newer lifecycle behavior.\n2. Should failed finalizers leave a small local retry/error record under `.git/conversation-ledger/`, deleted after success and retried by the next hook? I recommend yes. The simpler alternative is silent best-effort failure plus the existing next-session sweep.\n\nYou can answer “use both recommendations.”\n\nOnce the Claude PR exists, your required live test will be headless—no TUI inspection:\n\n```sh\nprobe=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ngit -C \"$probe\" init -q\ngit -C \"$probe\" config user.name TailProbe\ngit -C \"$probe\" config user.email tail@example.invalid\ntouch \"$probe/seed\"\ngit -C \"$probe\" add seed\ngit -C \"$probe\" commit -qm init\n\nmarker=\"$probe/started-at\"\ntouch \"$marker\"\n(cd \"$probe\" && claude -p 'Reply with exactly: tail-probe')\nsleep 12\n\ntranscript=\"$(\n  find \"$HOME/.claude/projects\" -type f -name '*.jsonl' \\\n    -newer \"$marker\" -print0 |\n  xargs -0 ls -t |\n  head -n 1\n)\"\n\nsession=\"${transcript##*/}\"\nsession=\"${session%.jsonl}\"\ncursor=\"$probe/.git/conversation-ledger/cursors/$session.json\"\n\nnode -e '\nconst fs = require(\"fs\");\nconst [cursor, transcript] = process.argv.slice(1);\nconst saved = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(cursor, \"utf8\")).size;\nconst actual = fs.statSync(transcript).size;\nconsole.log({saved, actual, complete: saved === actual});\nprocess.exit(saved === actual ? 0 : 1);\n' \"$cursor\" \"$transcript\"\n\n(cd \"$probe\" && cledger capture claude-code --transcript \"$transcript\")\n```\n\nExpected results:\n\n- `complete: true`\n- Manual recapture reports `+0 events`\n\nThe adjacent Annals checkout currently contains unrelated in-progress modifications, so I preserved it rather than overwriting them. The shared cledger checkout is clean and fast-forwarded to merged `main`.","type":"Text"}],"id":"msg_0f003e6fd7c49f3a016ab5d36bab4087d28970f7a5ae1bff24","phase":"final_answer","type":"AgentMessage"},"started_at_ms":1790301035676,"thread_id":"01a0d132-3af5-7f63-9