{"content":{"activity_type":"event_msg/item_completed","completed_at_ms":1790302626078,"item":{"content":[{"text":"Yes—the delay is acceptable, and I no longer recommend building the detached finalizer now.\n\nI attempted the headless tests myself, but both installed CLIs lack non-interactive authentication. Instead, I safely inspected only record types in a real currently-uncaptured Claude tail. It contained six records:\n\n- attachment metadata\n- stop-hook summary\n- turn duration\n- last-prompt metadata\n- two cost-state records\n\nThere were no missing user or assistant turns. Historical evidence shows the tail can also contain a more complete file-history snapshot, which is useful for forensic reconstruction but not essential to the conversation record.\n\nThe existing behavior is therefore reasonable:\n\n- Completed turns are captured immediately by `Stop`.\n- Closing bookkeeping may remain local temporarily.\n- The next `Stop` or `SessionEnd` in the same project sweeps the prior tracked transcript.\n- Manual capture remains available if needed.\n- The meaningful risk is only if you never use that project again and then lose/delete its original transcript before recovery.\n\nAdding detached workers, persistent jobs, anchor preservation, retry handling, and cross-platform process management is disproportionate to preserving metadata a little sooner.\n\nI recommend reclassifying this from an active TODO to an accepted eventual-consistency limitation, with a reconsideration trigger only if we observe substantive turns—or uniquely valuable content—being stranded. One correction to my earlier wording: merely opening Claude/Qwen is not enough; a subsequent hook in that project must fire, normally after completing a turn or ending the new session.","type":"Text"}],"id":"msg_0f003e6fd7c49f3a016ab5d99c3e1487d2bce07a8046c2c20d","phase":"final_answer","type":"AgentMessage"},"started_at_ms":1790302620263,"thread_id":"01a0d132-3af5-7f63-9ce8-f92787b93442","turn_id":"01a0d