de.ts:616:export async function captureQwenAll(cwd: string, limit?: number): Promise<CaptureResult> {\nsrc/adapters/qwen-code.ts:656:async function sweepStaleTranscripts(\nsrc/adapters/qwen-code.ts:689:    mergeCaptureResult(total, await sweepStaleTranscripts(repo, cwd, seen));\nsrc/adapters/claude-code.ts:607:async function sweepStaleTranscripts(\nsrc/adapters/claude-code.ts:646:    await sweepStaleTranscripts(payload.transcript_path, cwd, seen);\nsrc/adapters/claude-code.ts:688:   * purpose (see `sweepStaleTranscripts`), which is exactly where torn lines\nsrc/adapters/claude-code.ts:960:export async function captureClaudeAll(cwd: string, limit?: number): Promise<CaptureResult> {\nREADME.md:47:`cledger install` adds `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks to `~/.claude/settings.json`,\nREADME.md:48:a `Stop` hook (plus `features.hooks = true`) to `~/.codex/config.toml`, and a\nREADME.md:228:| Claude Code CLI | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl` | Also covers the VS Code extension and JetBrains plugin (both share `~/.claude/settings.json` hooks and transcripts), and desktop-app local/SSH/WSL sessions. Cloud \"Remote\" sessions and the Cowork tab run server-side — not captured. Every line type is now recorded: `system` lines that printed text become turns spoken by the system, `attachment` becomes `context_injection`, `mode`/`permission-mode`/`worktree-state`/`pr-link` and friends become `session_state`, `queue-operation` and the rest become `activity`, and `file-history-*` becomes `file_snapshot` with backup digests resolved at capture time. Sidechain (subagent) turns are captured as sub-conversations instead of dropped — including the `<session>/subagents/agent-*.jsonl` sibling files newer Claude Code writes, which the hook payload never names. The hook also sweeps other transcripts in the same project that cledger already has a cursor for and whose file grew past it, which is how a session's final lines get captured at all: the last hook reads to EOF, and Claude Code writes the closing bookkeeping (including the session's most complete file-history snapshot) afterwards. A transcript with no cursor is left alone — it was never cledger's to capture, and adopting it would backfill old conversations against today's HEAD. Run `cledger capture claude-code --all` from the exact directory used to start Claude to adopt those sessions deliberately; it follows Claude's realpath/NFC project naming and verifies each transcript's recorded `cwd` before capture. |\nREADME.md:229:| Codex CLI | `[[hooks.Stop]]` hooks engine | `~/.codex/sessions/**/rollout-*.jsonl` | Same config + session store is shared by the Codex desktop app and IDE extension, so their local sessions should capture too — but OpenAI has open bugs on the desktop app's config loading, and third-party reports say hooks may not fire from IDE sessions. Treat non-CLI surfaces as best-effort; `cledger capture codex` backfills any rollout file regardless of which surface wrote it. Cloud tasks run server-side — not captured. Provider-encrypted `reasoning` items are preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events (hidden from `log`/`show` by default, see Roadmap); inter-agent messages (`agent_message`) are captured as two events at the same `seq`: the visible turn, and a sealed `reasoning`-kind sibling carrying the embedded `encrypted_content` blocks, so an inter-agent session can still be replayed back through the provider that encrypted it. Sub-agent threads get their own rollout file, indistinguishable by name, and are captured recursively as sub-conversations of the thread that spawned them by following the child thread id in sub-agent activity records (a session is keyed on `session_meta.payload.id`, since a child's `session_id` field names its *parent*). `turn_context` and `session_meta` are now recorded as `session_state` as well as read for `producer` metadata, so the sandbox policy, approval policy and reasoning effort a turn ran under are part of the record. Of the `event_msg` UI stream, only `agent_message`/`user_message` are dropped — they genuinely duplicate a `response_item`; token counts, task lifecycle, sub-agent activity, patch application and aborts have no twin and are recorded as `activity`. |\nREADME.md:231:| Gemini CLI | `AfterAgent`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl` | The session file is append-only but is *not* a transcript: it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document — `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence but deliberately does **not** honour the removals: verified on a live session, Gemini rewrote the document after a failed API call and silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed, so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value, and records each withdrawal as an `activity` naming the ids it took out — `rewind` for an explicit `$rewindTo`, `snapshot_drop` for a `$set.messages` snapshot that quietly omits what the document used to hold — so a withdrawn turn is legible as withdrawn rather than merely present. Both paths matter: the snapshot is the one the live incident above actually took. Verified against live Gemini CLI 0.54.0 sessions — including one where Gemini **deleted its own session file** after a `/rewind`, leaving the ledger's copy of that conversation the only surviving record of it. `$set` patches become `session_state` (with the message list left out of `raw`, since Gemini rewrites the *whole* conversation into every snapshot and each message is already captured as its own event), `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Because nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting and never reused, so a rewind cannot renumber later turns — the positional churn the opencode adapter accepts does not arise here. A model turn whose tool calls have not settled is withheld and holds the cursor back. Sub-sessions filed under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` are captured as sub-conversations. Gemini's harness-authored `<session_context>`/`<hook_context>` preambles are recorded but attributed to `system`, not to you. |\nREADME.md:232:| Qwen Code | `Stop`/`SessionEnd` hooks | `~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl` | A fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so capture is per line with a line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, and the same catch-up sweep claude-code runs. Content is a Google GenAI `Part` list (shared with the Gemini CLI adapter): a `thought` part becomes a visible `thinking` block, `functionCall`/`functionResponse` become `tool_use`/`tool_result`. Everything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`, and each maps to a kind: `attribution_snapshot` becomes `file_snapshot` (with per-file content hashes already in the record, so nothing needs resolving against a local cache), `custom_title`/`session_source`/`parent_session`/`goal_state` become `session_state`, `at_command` and the agent-launch prompts become `context_injection`, and everything else — including a subtype a future Qwen adds — becomes `activity` rather than being dropped or guessed at. A `parent_session` record makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation of the session that spawned it — though on Qwen Code 0.21.5 that is **not** how delegation was observed to work: a subagent runs in-process behind an `agent` tool call, writes no chat file of its own and no `parent_session` record, so the ledger stores the delegation as an ordinary `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair in the parent. Nothing about it is lost — the prompt handed to the subagent, its status, terminate reason and a per-tool execution summary all ride in `toolCallResult.resultDisplay` and are preserved in `raw` — but the subagent's own turn-by-turn conversation is never written by Qwen at all, so no adapter can recover it. Assistant lines state the serving model; `provider` is left unset because Qwen records only an *auth mode* (`openai`, `qwen-oauth`), not the inference provider the schema asks for. |\nREADME.md:421:  `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`). `cledger install` wrote Claude's `120` into all\nREADME.md:1030:  `SessionEnd`-with-delay would still close the final-tail gap properly.\n   580\t *\n   581\t * This exists because a session's own last lines are unreachable to it. The\n   582\t * hook fires, capture reads to end-of-file, and Claude Code then writes more\n   583\t * — the closing bookkeeping of the final turn, and crucially the session's\n   584\t * last `file-history-snapshot`, which is the most complete one it will ever\n   585\t * write. No further hook fires for that session, so those lines sat stranded\n   586\t * behind a cursor nothing would ever advance again. Observed live: a cursor\n   587\t * resting at line 40 of a 50-line transcript, with the only snapshot naming a\n   588\t * real backup at line 49.\n   589\t *\n   590\t * The next session in the same project closes the gap. Staleness is decided\n   591\t * by file size against the size recorded with the cursor, so the sweep stats\n   592\t * each transcript rather than parsing it, and reads only the ones that grew.\n   593\t *\n   594\t * A transcript with *no* cursor is skipped, and that bound is the whole point\n   595\t * of the pass. A missing cursor does not mean \"this session has a gap\", it\n   596\t * means cledger never captured this session at all — it predates the install,\n   597\t * or the user did not want it. Adopting those would turn a gap-closing sweep\n   598\t * into a silent backfill of every conversation ever held in the repo, and\n   599\t * would anchor each one to whatever HEAD happens to be checked out now rather\n   600\t * than to the commit it was actually about. A session cledger already tracks\n   601\t * is the only session it has any business finishing.\n   602\t *\n   603\t * Failures are swallowed per session: a sweep is a bonus pass over\n   604\t * conversations the user is not currently having, and must never be the\n   605\t * reason their live capture reports an error.\n   606\t */\n   607\tasync function sweepStaleTranscripts(\n   608\t  transcriptPath: string,\n   609\t  cwd: string,\n   610\t  seen: Set<string>,\n   611\t): Promise<void> {\n   612\t  const repo = await findRepo(cwd);\n   613\t  if (!repo) return;\n   614\t  let entries: string[];\n   615\t  try {\n   616\t    entries = await readdir(dirname(transcriptPath));\n   617\t  } catch {\n   618\t    return;\n   619\t  }\n   620\t\n   621\t  for (const name of entries) {\n   622\t    if (!name.endsWith(\".jsonl\")) continue;\n   623\t    const path = join(dirname(transcriptPath), name);\n   624\t    if (seen.has(path)) continue;\n   625\t    try {\n   626\t      const cursor = await readCursor(repo, basename(name, \".jsonl\"), CURSOR_FIELD);\n   627\t      if (cursor === null) continue; // never captured — not this pass's business\n   628\t      const info = await stat(path);\n   629\t      if (info.size <= cursor.size) continue; // nothing new since the last read\n   630\t      await captureClaudeTranscript(path, cwd, seen);\n   631\t    } catch {\n   632\t      continue;\n   633\t    }\n   634\t  }\n   635\t}\n   636\t\n   637\texport async function runClaudeCodeHook(stdinJson: string): Promise<void> {\n   638\t  try {\n   639\t    const payload = JSON.parse(stdinJson) as ClaudeCodeHookPayload;\n   640\t    const cwd = payload.cwd ?? process.cwd();\n   641\t    if (!payload.transcript_path) return;\n   642\t    const repo = await findRepo(cwd);\n   643\t    if (!repo) return; // hooks must never break the user's session outside a repo\n   644\t    const seen = new Set<string>();\n   645\t    await captureClaudeTranscript(payload.transcript_path, cwd, seen);\n   646\t    await sweepStaleTranscripts(payload.transcript_path, cwd, seen);\n   647\t  } catch (err) {\n   648\t    process.stderr.write(\n   649\t      `cledger: claude-code hook error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}\\n`,\n   650\t    );\n   651\t  }\n   652\t}\n   653\t\n   654\t/**\n   655\t * Capture one transcript file. Does not print, does not follow subagents —\n   656\t * `captureClaudeTranscript` wraps this to do both.\n   657\t */\n   658\tasync function captureTranscriptFile(\n   659\t  repo: RepoInfo,\n   660\t  transcriptPath: string,\n   661\t): Promise<CaptureResult> {\n   662\t  const result: CaptureResult = { appended: 0, deduped: 0, unrecognized: {} };\n   663\t  let raw: string;\n   664\t  try {\n   665\t    raw = await readFile(transcriptPath, \"utf8\");\n   666\t  } catch {\n   667\t    return result; // transcript not written yet\n   668\t  }\n   669\t  // Whether the writer had finished the last line when we read. A transcript\n   670\t  // that does not end in a newline was caught mid-write, which is how the\n   671\t  // final line comes to be torn — see `tornTail` below.\n   672\t  const endsWithNewline = raw.endsWith(\"\\n\");\n   673\t  const lines = raw.split(\"\\n\");\n   674\t  if (lines.length > 0 && lines[lines.length - 1] === \"\") lines.pop();\n   675\t\n   676\t  const sessionId = basename(transcriptPath, \".jsonl\");\n   677\t  const stored = (await readCursor(repo, sessionId, CURSOR_FIELD)) ?? { count: 0, size: 0 };\n   678\t  let cursor = stored.count;\n   679\t  if (cursor > lines.length) cursor = 0; // transcript is shorter than expected — rescan from the start\n   680\t\n   681\t  /**\n   682\t   * Index of a half-written final line, when there is one.\n   683\t   *\n   684\t   * A line that fails to parse is normally a lost cause and gets skipped, but\n   685\t   * the last line of a file still being written is a different thing: the\n   686\t   * bytes are on their way. Advancing the cursor past it would skip it\n   687\t   * permanently, and this adapter now goes looking for transcript tails on\n   688\t   * purpose (see `sweepStaleTranscripts`), which is exactly where torn lines\n   689\t   * live. So the cursor stops short of it and the next read picks it up whole.\n   690\t   *\n   691\t   * The two conditions together are what make this safe: a parse failure\n   692\t   * anywhere but the unterminated last line is real corruption, and holding\n   693\t   * the cursor at it would stall the session's capture forever.\n   694\t   */\n   695\t  let tornTail: number | null = null;\n   696\t\n   697\t  const version = packageVersion();\n   698\t  const identity = await gitUserIdentity(repo);\n   699\t  let baseTime: string | null = null; // computed lazily, only if a timestampless unrecognized line needs it\n   700\t  const drafts: EventDraft[] = [];\n   701\t  for (let i = cursor; i < lines.length; i++) {\n   702\t    const text = lines[i]!;\n   703\t    if (!text.trim()) continue;\n   704\t    let parsed: ClaudeTranscriptLine;\n   705\t    try {\n   706\t      parsed = JSON.parse(text) as ClaudeTranscriptLine;\n   707\t    } catch {\n   708\t      // Partial line — normal at the tail of a live transcript. Remember it\n   709\t      // so the cursor stops here rather than stepping over it for good.\n   710\t      if (!endsWithNewline && i === lines.length - 1) tornTail = i;\n   711\t      continue;\n   712\t    }\n   713\t    const type = typeof parsed.type === \"string\" ? parsed.type : \"(untyped)\";\n   714\t    if (!CONVERTIBLE_LINE_TYPES.has(type)) {\n   715\t      // Most non-turn lines carry no timestamp of their own, so the session\n   716\t      // base time is now needed on the common path rather than the rare one;\n   717\t      // it stays lazy so a transcript of nothing but turns never computes it.\n   718\t      if (baseTime === null) baseTime = firstTimestamp(lines) ?? (await sessionMtime(transcriptPath));\n   719\t      const occurredAt = typeof parsed.timestamp === \"string\" ? parsed.timestamp : baseTime;\n   720\t      const ctx = recordContext(parsed, occurredAt, i, sessionId, version);\n   630\t    mergeCaptureResult(total, await captureTranscriptFile(repo, path));\n   631\t  }\n   632\t  process.stderr.write(`cledger: qwen-code +${total.appended} events (${total.deduped} deduped)\\n`);\n   633\t  warnUnrecognized(\"qwen-code\", total.unrecognized);\n   634\t  return total;\n   635\t}\n   636\t\n   637\t/**\n   638\t * Capture any *other* transcript in this project that has grown since it was\n   639\t * last read — the same gap-closing pass the claude-code adapter runs, and for\n   640\t * the same reason: a session's own final lines are unreachable to it, because\n   641\t * the hook fires, capture reads to end-of-file, and Qwen Code then writes the\n   642\t * closing bookkeeping of that turn with no further hook to pick it up.\n   643\t *\n   644\t * A transcript with *no* cursor is skipped, and that bound is the point. A\n   645\t * missing cursor means cledger never captured that session at all — it\n   646\t * predates the install, or the user did not want it — and adopting those\n   647\t * would turn a gap-closing sweep into a silent backfill of every conversation\n   648\t * ever held in the project, anchored to whatever HEAD is checked out now\n   649\t * rather than the commit it was about. `--all` is where that belongs, on\n   650\t * purpose and by request.\n   651\t *\n   652\t * Failures are swallowed per session: a sweep is a bonus pass over\n   653\t * conversations the user is not currently having, and must never be the\n   654\t * reason their live capture reports an error.\n   655\t */\n   656\tasync function sweepStaleTranscripts(\n   657\t  repo: RepoInfo,\n   658\t  cwd: string,\n   659\t  seen: Set<string>,\n   660\t): Promise<CaptureResult> {\n   661\t  const total: CaptureResult = { appended: 0, deduped: 0, unrecognized: {} };\n   662\t  for (const path of await projectTranscripts(cwd)) {\n   663\t    if (seen.has(path)) continue;\n   664\t    try {\n   665\t      const cursor = await readCursor(repo, basename(path, \".jsonl\"), CURSOR_FIELD);\n   666\t      if (cursor === null) continue; // never captured — not this pass's business\n   667\t      const info = await stat(path);\n   668\t      if (info.size <= cursor.size) continue; // nothing new since the last read\n   669\t      mergeCaptureResult(total, await captureTranscriptFile(repo, path));\n   670\t    } catch {\n   671\t      continue;\n   672\t    }\n   673\t  }\n   674\t  return total;\n   675\t}\n   676\t\n   677\texport async function runQwenHook(stdinJson: string): Promise<void> {\n   678\t  try {\n   679\t    const payload = JSON.parse(stdinJson) as QwenHookPayload;\n   680\t    const cwd = payload.cwd ?? process.cwd();\n   681\t    const repo = await findRepo(cwd);\n   682\t    if (!repo) return; // hooks must never break the user's session outside a repo\n   683\t    const total: CaptureResult = { appended: 0, deduped: 0, unrecognized: {} };\n   684\t    const seen = new Set<string>();\n   685\t    if (payload.transcript_path) {\n   686\t      seen.add(payload.transcript_path);\n   687\t      mergeCaptureResult(total, await captureTranscriptFile(repo, payload.transcript_path));\n   688\t    }\n   689\t    mergeCaptureResult(total, await sweepStaleTranscripts(repo, cwd, seen));\n   690\t    process.stderr.write(`cledger: qwen-code +${total.appended} events (${total.deduped} deduped)\\n`);\n   691\t    warnUnrecognized(\"qwen-code\", total.unrecognized);\n   692\t  } catch (err) {\n   693\t    process.stderr.write(\n   694\t      `cledger: qwen-code hook error: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}\\n`,\n   695\t    );\n   696\t  }\n   697\t}\n     1\timport { execFile } from \"node:child_process\";\n     2\timport { copyFile, mkdir, readFile, writeFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n     3\timport { existsSync } from \"node:fs\";\n     4\timport { homedir } from \"node:os\";\n     5\timport { dirname, join } from \"node:path\";\n     6\timport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n     7\timport { promisify } from \"node:util\";\n     8\timport { hasAuthorIdentity } from \"./transport.js\";\n     9\t\n    10\tconst execFileP = promisify(execFile);\n    11\t\n    12\t/**\n    13\t * Hook commands prefer the bare `cledger` binary so installs survive this\n    14\t * repo moving; when it isn't on PATH yet we fall back to an absolute\n    15\t * node+script invocation so hooks work immediately after `cledger install`.\n    16\t */\n    17\tasync function hookCommand(source: string): Promise<string> {\n    18\t  try {\n    19\t    await execFileP(\"cledger\", [\"--version\"]);\n    20\t    return `cledger hook ${source}`;\n    21\t  } catch {\n    22\t    const script = fileURLToPath(new URL(\"./cli.js\", import.meta.url));\n    23\t    return `\"${process.execPath}\" \"${script}\" hook ${source}`;\n    24\t  }\n    25\t}\n    26\t\n    27\t/**\n    28\t * The same invocation as `hookCommand`, but as an argv array: the opencode\n    29\t * integration is a JS plugin that spawns the hook directly rather than a\n    30\t * shell command string in a config file.\n    31\t */\n    32\tasync function hookArgv(source: string): Promise<string[]> {\n    33\t  try {\n    34\t    await execFileP(\"cledger\", [\"--version\"]);\n    35\t    return [\"cledger\", \"hook\", source];\n    36\t  } catch {\n    37\t    const script = fileURLToPath(new URL(\"./cli.js\", import.meta.url));\n    38\t    return [process.execPath, script, \"hook\", source];\n    39\t  }\n    40\t}\n    41\t\n    42\tasync function backup(path: string): Promise<void> {\n    43\t  if (!existsSync(path)) return;\n    44\t  const stamp = new Date().toISOString().replace(/[:.]/g, \"-\");\n    45\t  await copyFile(path, `${path}.bak-${stamp}`);\n    46\t}\n    47\t\n    48\tinterface ClaudeHookEntry {\n    49\t  matcher?: string;\n    50\t  hooks: { type: string; command?: string; timeout?: number }[];\n    51\t}\n    52\t\n    53\t/** The cledger hook inside an event's entries, if it is already installed. */\n    54\tfunction findCledgerHook(\n    55\t  entries: ClaudeHookEntry[] | undefined,\n    56\t  needle: string,\n    57\t): { type: string; command?: string; timeout?: number } | undefined {\n    58\t  for (const entry of entries ?? []) {\n    59\t    const hook = entry.hooks?.find((h) => h.command?.includes(needle));\n    60\t    if (hook) return hook;\n    61\t  }\n    62\t  return undefined;\n    63\t}\n    64\t\n    65\t/**\n    66\t * How long a hook may run before the CLI kills it — in whatever unit that CLI\n    67\t * reads the field in, which is *not* the same across the three.\n    68\t *\n    69\t * Claude Code reads `timeout` as **seconds**. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code forked\n    70\t * Claude's hook config format but not its unit: both pass the number straight\n    71\t * to `setTimeout`, so theirs is **milliseconds**. Their own code says so —\n    72\t * `Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`, with `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`.\n    73\t *\n    74\t * Writing Claude's `120` into their settings therefore asks to be SIGTERMed\n    75\t * after 120 *milliseconds*. A cold capture is ~85ms of work on top of node's\n    76\t * startup, so the hook loses that race nearly every time: Gemini reported\n    77\t * \"Hook(s) [...] failed\" on almost every turn while its events sometimes still\n    78\t * landed, because whether `appendEvents` finished before the signal was a coin\n    79\t * flip. Diagnosed by trapping the signal in a wrapper script — the hook logged\n    80\t * `caught SIGTERM` in the same second it started.\n    81\t */\n    82\tconst HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 120;\n    83\tconst HOOK_TIMEOUT_MILLISECONDS = 120_000;\n    84\t\n    85\t/**\n    86\t *