{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.7 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Hooks reference - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"pattern\":\"How async hooks execute\"}); Total lines: 3654\nL132:     * cite123†PreModelSwitch decision control L133:     * cite124†PostModelSwitch L134:     * cite125†PostModelSwitch input L135:     * cite126†PostModelSwitch decision control L136:     * cite127†SessionEnd L137:     * cite128†SessionEnd input L138:     * cite129†Elicitation L139:     * cite130†Elicitation input L140:     * cite131†Elicitation output L141:     * cite132†ElicitationResult L142:     * cite133†ElicitationResult input L143:     * cite134†ElicitationResult output L147:     * cite138†Response schema L148:     * cite139†Check multiple conditions before stopping L149:   * cite140†Agent-based hooks L150:     * cite141†How agent hooks work L151:     * cite142†Agent hook configuration L152:   * cite143†Run hooks in the background L153:     * cite144†Configure an async hook L154:     * cite145†How async hooks execute L155:     * cite146†Run tests after file changes L156:     * cite147†Limitations L157:   * cite148†Security considerations L158:     * cite149†Disclaimer L159:     * cite150†Workspace trust L160:     * cite151†Security best practices L161:   * cite152†Windows PowerShell tool L162:   * cite153†Debug hooks L163: Reference\nL164: # Hooks reference\nL165: \nL166: Reference for Claude Code hook events, configuration schema, JSON input/output formats, exit codes, async hooks, HTTP hooks, prompt hooks, and MCP tool hooks.\nL167: \nL168: Copy page Copy page\nL169: \nL170: For a quickstart guide with examples, see cite154†Automate actions with hooks .\nL171: Hooks are user-defined shell commands, HTTP endpoints, MCP tool calls, LLM prompts, or subagents that execute automatically at specific points in Claude Code’s lifecycle. Claude Code fires the same hook events wherever it runs: sessions in the terminal, IDE extensions, the cite155†Desktop app , and cite156†cloud sessions . Use this reference to look up event schemas, configuration options, JSON input/output formats, and advanced features like async hooks, HTTP hooks, and MCP tool hooks.\nL172: ##\nL173: \nL174: cite11†​ L175: \nL176: Hook lifecycle\nL177: \nL178: Claude Code runs hooks at specific points during a session. When an event fires and a matcher matches, Claude Code passes JSON context about the event to your hook handler. For command hooks, input arrives on stdin. For HTTP hooks, it arrives as the POST request body. Your handler can then inspect the input, take action, and optionally return a decision. Events fall into three cadences:\nL3447: ##\nL3448: \nL3449: cite143†​ L3450: \nL3451: Run hooks in the background\nL3452: \nL3453: By default, hooks block Claude’s execution until they complete. For long-running tasks like deployments, test suites, or external API calls, set `\"async\": true` to run the hook in the background while Claude continues working. Async hooks can’t block or control Claude’s behavior: response fields like `decision`, `permissionDecision`, and `continue` have no effect, because the action they would have controlled has already completed.\nL3478: Once an async hook is running in the background, Claude Code doesn’t enforce `timeout` on it. Claude Code still enforces `timeout` on a hook you run with `asyncRewake`. Claude Code delivers an async hook’s results only while the session runs:\nL3479: \nL3480:   * In cite206†non-interactive mode with the `-p` flag, Claude Code kills any async hook still running at teardown and finalizes it with outcome `cancelled`\nL3481:   * If your hook’s work must outlive a `claude -p` session, start a fully detached process from it\nL3482: ###\nL3483: \nL3484: cite145†​ L3485: \nL3486: How async hooks execute\nL3487: When an async hook fires, Claude Code starts the hook process and immediately continues without waiting for it to finish. The hook receives the same JSON input via stdin as a synchronous hook. After the background process exits, Claude Code delivers the `additionalContext` and `systemMessage` fields from the hook’s JSON response to Claude on the next conversation turn. Unlike a synchronous hook’s `systemMessage`, neither field is shown to you.\nL3488: Claude Code validates that JSON response against the same cite36†output schema as synchronous hooks, and drops any field whose value has the wrong type, such as a `systemMessage` that isn’t a string, instead of delivering it. Run with `--debug` to see a warning naming each dropped field. Before v2.1.202, malformed JSON output from an async hook could crash the session, and the crash recurred each time the session was resumed. Async hook completion notifications are suppressed by default.\nL3540: ###\nL3541: \nL3542: cite147†​ L3543: \nL3544: Limitations\nL3545: \nL3546: Async hooks have additional constraints compared to synchronous hooks:\nL3547: \nL3548:   * Hook output is delivered on the next conversation turn. If the session is idle, the response waits until the next user interaction. Exception: an `asyncRewake` hook that exits with code 2 wakes Claude immediately even when the session is idle.\nL3549:   * Each execution creates a separate background process. There is no deduplication across multiple firings of the same async hook.\nL3550: ##\nL3551: \nL3552: cite148†​ L3553: \nL3554: Security considerations\nL3555: \nL3556: ###\nL3557: \nL3558: cite149†​ L3559: \nL3560: Disclaimer\nL3561: \nL3562: Command hooks execute shell commands with your full user permissions. They can modify, delete, or access any files your user account can access. Review and test all hook commands before adding them to your configuration.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHooks reference - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"pattern\":\"Async hooks do not run on SessionEnd\"}); Total lines: 3654\nL132:     * cite123†PreModelSwitch decision control L133:     * cite124†PostModelSwitch L134:     * cite125†PostModelSwitch input L135:     * cite126†PostModelSwitch decision control L136:     * cite127†SessionEnd L137:     * cite128†SessionEnd input L138:     * cite129†Elicitation L139:     * cite130†Elicitation input L140:     * cite131†Elicitation output L141:     * cite132†ElicitationResult L142:     * cite133†ElicitationResult input L143:     * cite134†ElicitationResult output L144:   * cite135†Prompt-based hooks L145:     * cite136†How prompt-based hooks work L146:     * cite137†Prompt hook configuration L147:     * cite138†Response schema L148:     * cite139†Check multiple conditions before stopping L149:   * cite140†Agent-based hooks L150:     * cite141†How agent hooks work L151:     * cite142†Agent hook configuration L152:   * cite143†Run hooks in the background L153:     * cite144†Configure an async hook L154:     * cite145†How async hooks execute L155:     * cite146†Run tests after file changes L156:     * cite147†Limitations L157:   * cite148†Security considerations L158:     * cite149†Disclaimer L159:     * cite150†Workspace trust L160:     * cite151†Security best practices L161:   * cite152†Windows PowerShell tool L162:   * cite153†Debug hooks L163: Reference\nL164: # Hooks reference\nL165: \nL166: Reference for Claude Code hook events, configuration schema, JSON input/output formats, exit codes, async hooks, HTTP hooks, prompt hooks, and MCP tool hooks.\nL167: \nL168: Copy page Copy page\nL169: \nL170: For a quickstart guide with examples, see cite154†Automate actions with hooks .\nL171: Hooks are user-defined shell commands, HTTP endpoints, MCP tool calls, LLM prompts, or subagents that execute automatically at specific points in Claude Code’s lifecycle. Claude Code fires the same hook events wherever it runs: sessions in the terminal, IDE extensions, the cite155†Desktop app , and cite156†cloud sessions . Use this reference to look up event schemas, configuration options, JSON input/output formats, and advanced features like async hooks, HTTP hooks, and MCP tool hooks.\nL172: ##\nL173: \nL174: cite11†​ L175: \nL176: Hook lifecycle\nL177: \nL178: Claude Code runs hooks at specific points during a session. When an event fires and a matcher matches, Claude Code passes JSON context about the event to your hook handler. For command hooks, input arrives on stdin. For HTTP hooks, it arrives as the POST request body. Your handler can then inspect the input, take action, and optionally return a decision. Events fall into three cadences:\nL179:   * per session: `SessionStart` and `SessionEnd`\nL180:   * per turn: `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, and `StopFailure`\nL181:   * on every tool call inside the agentic loop: `PreToolUse` and `PostToolUse`, except cite157†`EndConversation` calls, which skip both\nL182: cite158†Image: Hook lifecycle diagram showing optional Setup feeding into SessionStart, then a per-turn loop containing UserPromptSubmit, UserPromptExpansion for slash commands, the nested agentic loop (PreToolUse, PermissionRequest, PostToolUse, PostToolUseFailure, PostToolBatch, SubagentStart/Stop, TaskCreated, TaskCompleted), and Stop or StopFailure, followed by TeammateIdle, PreCompact, PostCompact, and SessionEnd, with Elicitation and ElicitationResult nested inside MCP tool execution, PermissionDenied as a side branch from PermissionRequest for auto-mode denials, WorktreeCreate, WorktreeRemove, Notification, ConfigChange, InstructionsLoaded, CwdChanged, FileChanged, and DirectoryAdded as standalone async events, PreModelSwitch as a standalone sequential event that runs before a requested model switch, PostModelSwitch as a standalone async event that runs after the session's model changes, and MessageDisplay as a display-only event that runs while assistant message text streams†mintcdn.com cite159†Image: Hook lifecycle diagram showing optional Setup feeding into SessionStart, then a per-turn loop containing UserPromptSubmit, UserPromptExpansion for slash commands, the nested agentic loop (PreToolUse, PermissionRequest, PostToolUse, PostToolUseFailure, PostToolBatch, SubagentStart/Stop, TaskCreated, TaskCompleted), and Stop or StopFailure, followed by TeammateIdle, PreCompact, PostCompact, and SessionEnd, with Elicitation and ElicitationResult nested inside MCP tool execution, PermissionDenied as a side branch from PermissionRequest for auto-mode denials, WorktreeCreate, WorktreeRemove, Notification, ConfigChange, InstructionsLoaded, CwdChanged, FileChanged, and DirectoryAdded as standalone async events, PreModelSwitch as a standalone sequential event that runs before a requested model switch, PostModelSwitch as a standalone async event that runs after the session's model changes, and MessageDisplay as a display-only event that runs while assistant message text streams†mintcdn.com L1284: Variables written to that file persist into subsequent Bash commands for the session, just as in cite44†SessionStart hooks . Only `type: \"command\"` hooks run on `Setup`. A `type: \"mcp_tool\"` hook on `Setup` is always skipped, as described under cite21†MCP tool hook fields .\nL1661: ##### WebFetch\nL1662: \nL1663: Fetches and processes web content.\nL1664: \nL1665: Field  | Type  | Example  | Description\nL1666: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL1667: `url`  | string  | `\"https://example.com/api\"`  | URL to fetch content from\nL1668: `prompt`  | string  | `\"Extract the API endpoints\"`  | Prompt to run on the fetched content\nL2392: ###\nL2393: \nL2394: cite93†​ L2395: \nL2396: Stop\nL2397: \nL2398: Runs when the main Claude Code agent has finished responding. Does not run if the stoppage occurred due to a user interrupt. API errors fire cite96†StopFailure instead.\nL2399: \nL2400: The cite258†`/goal` command is a built-in shortcut for a session-scoped prompt-based Stop hook. Use it when you want Claude to keep working toward a condition without writing hook configuration.\nL3377:   * `PermissionDenied`: `ok: false` has no effect because the denial already happened. The only output this event reads is `hookSpecificOutput.retry`, which prompt and agent hooks can’t set. They run on this event, but their output is discarded. Use a cite19†command hook to return `retry`\nL3378: If you need finer control on any event, use a cite19†command hook with the per-event fields described in cite39†Decision control .\nL3447: ##\nL3448: \nL3449: cite143†​ L3450: \nL3451: Run hooks in the background\nL3452: \nL3453: By default, hooks block Claude’s execution until they complete. For long-running tasks like deployments, test suites, or external API calls, set `\"async\": true` to run the hook in the background while Claude continues working. Async hooks can’t block or control Claude’s behavior: response fields like `decision`, `permissionDecision`, and `continue` have no effect, because the action they would have controlled has already completed.\nL3459: Add `\"async\": true` to a command hook’s configuration to run it in the background without blocking Claude. This field is only available on `type: \"command\"` hooks. This hook runs a test script after every `Write` tool call. Claude continues working immediately while `run-tests.sh` executes. When the script finishes, its output is delivered on the next conversation turn:\nL3460: \nL3461:     `{\nL3462:       \"hooks\": {\nL3463:         \"PostToolUse\": [\nL3464:           {\nL3465:             \"matcher\": \"Write\",\nL3466:             \"hooks\": [\nL3467:               {\nL3468:                 \"type\": \"command\",\nL3469:                 \"command\": \"/path/to/run-tests.sh\",\nL3470:                 \"async\": true\nL3471:               }\nL3472:             ]\nL3473:           }\nL3474:         ]\nL3475:       }\nL3476:     }\nL3477:     `\nL3478: Once an async hook is running in the background, Claude Code doesn’t enforce `timeout` on it. Claude Code still enforces `timeout` on a hook you run with `asyncRewake`. Claude Code delivers an async hook’s results only while the session runs:\nL3479: \nL3480:   * In cite206†non-interactive mode with the `-p` flag, Claude Code kills any async hook still running at teardown and finalizes it with outcome `cancelled`\nL3481:   * If your hook’s work must outlive a `claude -p` session, start a fully detached process from it\nL3482: ###\nL3483: \nL3484: cite145†​ L3485: \nL3486: How async hooks execute\nL3487: When an async hook fires, Claude Code starts the hook process and immediately continues without waiting for it to finish. The hook receives the same JSON input via stdin as a synchronous hook. After the background process exits, Claude Code delivers the `additionalContext` and `systemMessage` fields from the hook’s JSON response to Claude on the next conversation turn. Unlike a synchronous hook’s `systemMessage`, neither field is shown to you.\nL3488: Claude Code validates that JSON response against the same cite36†output schema as synchronous hooks, and drops any field whose value has the wrong type, such as a `systemMessage` that isn’t a string, instead of delivering it. Run with `--debug` to see a warning naming each dropped field. Before v2.1.202, malformed JSON output from an async hook could crash the session, and the crash recurred each time the session was resumed. Async hook completion notifications are suppressed by default.\nL3540: ###\nL3541: \nL3542: cite147†​ L3543: \nL3544: Limitations\nL3545: \nL3546: Async hooks have additional constraints compared to synchronous hooks:\nL3547: \nL3548:   * Hook output is delivered on the next conversation turn. If the session is idle, the response waits until the next user interaction. Exception: an `asyncRewake` hook that exits with code 2 wakes Claude immediately even when the session is idle.\nL3549:   * Each execution creates a separate background process. There is no deduplication across multiple firings of the same async hook.\n","type":"input_text"}],"tool_use_id":"call_l3vVHVDtz2gKQDZP4AoBCTnL","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"tool_result"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"a6821c75dddf42529c15a1402d89fb01aaae50a9","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-ba03395c5e8a2abe54c04fb636035a49f36aa8fcd3aaa4d59057cbb186a5e7df","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","provider":"openai","session_id":"01a0d132-3af5-7f63-9ce8-f92787b93442","source":"codex","source_version":"0.156.1"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-25T01:20:47.110Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000},"ordinal":1583,"payload":{"call_id":"call_l3vVHVDtz2gKQDZP4AoBCTnL","id":"ctco_01a0d626-9205-72f3-a148-337a2c9e1cb4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1790299247.109928,"turn_id":"01a0d625-2904-71a2-a1c3-4c118c742ef4"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.7 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Hooks reference - Claude Code Docs (https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"pattern\":\"How async hooks execute\"}); Total lines: 3654\nL132:     * cite123†PreModelSwitch decision control L133:     * cite124†PostModelSwitch L134:     * cite125†PostModelSwitch input L135:     * cite126†Post